# NH4PF6 assisted buried interface defect passivation for planar perovskite solar cells with efficiency exceeding 21%

## abstract
The buried interface defects severely affect the further enhancements of efficiency and stability of SnO2-based planar perovskite solar cells (PSCs). To well tackle this problem, we propose a passivation strategy employing NH4PF6 to modify the buried interface of perovskite layer ((FAPbI3)0.85(MAPbBr3)0.15 composition) in planar PSCs. After introducing NH4PF6, the oxygen defects on the surface of SnO2 film are greatly restricted due to the coordinate interaction between fluorine atoms (F) in PF6− and undercoordinated Sn4+. Meanwhile, the hydrogen bonding interaction (N–H⋯I) between NH4PF6 and PbI2 can passivate the non-radiative charge recombination sites, significantly optimizing the quality of perovskite film, as well as the charge transfer process at the SnO2/perovskite interface. As a result, the NH4PF6-modified PSC obtains a champion power conversion efficiency (PCE) of 21.11% superior to the reference device (18.46%), and the device with an active area of 1 cm2 achieves a PCE as high as 17.38%. Furthermore, the unencapsulated NH4PF6-modified PSCs show good humidity stability and retain about 80% of the initial PCE after 1080 h aging at the relative humidity (RH) of 35% ± 5%.

Abstract The buried interface defects severely affect the further enhancements of efficiency and stability of $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ - based planar perovskite solar cells (PSCs). To well tackle this problem, we propose a passivation strategy employing ${\mathrm{NH}}_{4}{\mathrm{PF}}_{6}$ to modify the buried interface of perovskite layer $((\mathrm{FAPbI_{3}})_{0.85}(\mathrm{MAPbBr_{3}})_{0.15}$ composition) in planar PSCs. After introducing ${\mathrm{NH}}_{4}{\mathrm{PF}}_{6}$ , the oxygen defects on the surface of $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ film are greatly restricted due to the coordinate interaction between fluorine atoms (F) in $\mathrm{PF}_{6}{}^{-}$ and undercoordinated $\mathrm{Sn}^{4+}$ . Meanwhile, the hydrogen bonding interaction $(\mathrm{N-H\cdot\cdot\cdotI})$ between ${\mathrm{NH}}_{4}{\mathrm{PF}}_{6}$ and $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ can passivate the non-radiative charge recombination sites, significantly optimizing the quality of perovskite film, as well as the charge transfer process at the $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/$ perovskite interface. As a result, the $\mathrm{NH_{4}P F_{6}}$ -modified PSC obtains a champion power conversion efficiency (PCE) of $21.11\%$ superior to the reference device $(18.46\%)$ , and the device with an active area of $1~\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ achieves a PCE as high as $17.38\%$ . Furthermore, the unencapsulated ${\mathrm{NH}}_{4}{\mathrm{PF}}_{6}$ -modified PSCs show good humidity stability and retain about
$80\%$ of the initial PCE after $1080{\mathrm{~h~}}$ aging at the relative humidity (RH) of $35\%\pm5\%$ .
Keywords Defect passivation; Interface engineering; Surface charge transfer; Stability; Perovskite solar cells (PSCs)
## 1 Introduction
After more than a decade of rapid development, perovskite solar cells (PSCs) have become one of the ‘‘super-stars’’ in the photovoltaic field [1–9]. To date, the record certified power conversion efficiency (PCE) of PSCs has achieved $25.7\%$ , which is close to the certified PCE of single-crystal silicon solar cells [10–14]. With the functions of electron extraction and transport, the electron transport layer (ETL) is an essential part of PSCs to achieve high PCE [15–17]. In addition, the ETL and ETL/perovskite interface have been recognized as non-negligible issues to affect the efficiency and stability of PSCs [18–21]. As an early proposed n-type semiconductor, $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ has been a mainstream ETL to fabricate PSCs for a long period [22, 23]. However, most of the preparation methods of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ thin film reported in the literature requires high-temperature sintering at about $500~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , which causes high energy consumption and is incompatible with flexible substrates [24]. Moreover, due to the high photocatalytic activity, $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ could lead to the instability of PSCs under ultraviolet (UV) irradiation, which limits its large-scale commercial application in the future [25, 26]. Tin dioxide $\left(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\right)$ is considered as an ideal substitute for $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ as ETL for PSCs. Compared with $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ film, $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ film possesses many advantages, such as low-temperature preparation, low hysteresis, excellent ultraviolet stability, better crystallinity, and higher electron mobility [27–30]. Hence, a dense $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ film could achieve more efficient charge transport and smaller recombination loss at the interface with perovskite [31]. Besides, the highest certified PCE of PSCs based on $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ as ETL reported in the literature has reached $25.5\%$ to date [21]. However, recent researches confirm that a large number of oxygen vacancies exist on the surface of $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ film prepared by commercial $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ hydrosol, which leads to instability and hysteresis of the devices [32–35]. Therefore, it is of vital importance to decrease the defects at $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ interface to further improve the performance of PSCs. Except for ETLs, the interface of ETL/perovskite also plays a crucial role in the photovoltaic performance of PSCs. Previous theoretical and experimental studies have shown that lattice mismatch at the heterojunction interface leads to a large quantity of the antisite and interstitial defects, inducing a high density of trap states [36–39]. Furthermore, these defects may lead to charge recombination or accumulation, ion migration, and permeation of oxygen or moisture into the perovskite films, which are responsible for the energy losses in open-circuit voltage $(V_{\mathrm{OC}})$ and the increased attenuation of the PSCs in ambient conditions [40]. Therefore, it is necessary to introduce inorganic/organic compounds into the ETL/perovskite interface to modulate the defects, thus minimizing the adverse impact on the photovoltaic performance of $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ - based planar PSCs [31, 41, 42].
To date, researchers have reported a number of strategies for passivating the defects at $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ ETL/perovskite interface [43, 44]. Zhang et al. reported a strategy to promote electron extraction of self-developed $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ nanocrystals facilitated by the formation of amorphous $\mathbf{Nb}{\mathbf O}_{x}$ $\mathrm{\langleSnO_{2}/N b O_{\it x}\rangle}$ ) external layer, achieving an impressive PCE of $24.01\%$ [45]. Liu et al. reported that the introduction of $\mathrm{{HCOONH_{4}}}$ could reduce the relaxation residual strain and micro-strain of perovskite films, and finally achieved the highest PCE of $22.37\%$ and certification of $21.9\%$ on flexible PSCs [46]. Liu et al. [47] reported that introducing $\mathrm{NH_{4}}^{+}$ into the ETL interface could inhibit surface charge recombination and enhance electron transport. It was reported that the introduction of $\mathrm{PF}_{6}{}^{-}$ formed hydrogen bond with perovskite and coordination bonds with $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ , which chemically connected ETL and perovskite layer, thus improving the interface contact [48–50]. In view of the excellent performance of $\mathrm{NH_{4}}^{+}$ and $\mathrm{PF}_{6}{}^{-}$ , it would be meaningful to combine $\mathrm{NH_{4}}^{+}$ and $\mathrm{PF}_{6}{}^{-}$ ions together to effectively inhibit oxygen vacancy on the $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ film and prevent the oxygen or moisture from deteriorating the perovskite film via defeat sites.
In this work, we introduced an ammonium hexafluorophosphate $\mathrm{(NH_{4}P F_{6})}$ salt as $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/$ perovskite interface modifier in PSCs. The ${\mathrm{NH}}_{4}{\mathrm{PF}}_{6}$ interface layer was spincoated on the top of the $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ film. Due to the coordinated interaction of fluorine atoms (F) in $\mathrm{PF}_{6}^{-}$ with undercoordinated $S\mathrm{n}^{4+}$ , the oxygen defects on the surface of $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ film were largely suppressed. In addition, the ${\mathrm{NH}}_{4}{\mathrm{PF}}_{6}$ could effectively compensate for the undercoordinated $\mathrm{I}^{-}$ at the buried surface of perovskite layer to reduce the nonradiative recombination of PSCs. Compared to the pristine $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ , the PSC with ${\mathrm{NH}}_{4}{\mathrm{PF}}_{6}$ modified $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ improved PCE from $18.46\%$ to $21.11\%$ , with maximum $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ up to $1.11\mathrm{~V~}$ (the reference is $1.03\mathrm{~V~}$ ). And, device with an active area of $\textstyle{1\ \mathrm{cm}^{2}}$ exhibited an efficiency of $17.38\%$ . Moreover, owing to ETL/perovskite surface deactivation, the ${\mathrm{NH}}_{4}{\mathrm{PF}}_{6}$ modified device achieved negligible hysteresis and significantly improved ambient stability. The unencapsulated ${\mathrm{NH}}_{4}{\mathrm{PF}}_{6}$ -modified PSCs demonstrated a good humidity stability, maintaining $80\%$ of the initial PCE at aging in ambient air after $1080{\mathrm{~h~}}$ . Therefore, the buried interface modification strategy with $\mathrm{NH_{4}P F_{6}}$ has been proved to be an effective avenue for $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ -based planar PSCs.
## 2 Results and discussion
The planar PSC with indium-tin-oxide $\mathrm{(ITO)}/\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/$ perovskite/Spiro-OMeTAD/Au structure was prepared, in which perovskite light harvesting material was (FAP$\mathbf{bI}_{3})_{0.85}(\mathbf{MAPbBr}_{3})_{0.15}$ . As shown in Fig. 1a, to further improve the photovoltaic performance of $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ -based PSCs, the ${\mathrm{NH}}_{4}{\mathrm{PF}}_{6}$ was introduced to modify the interface of $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/$ perovskite. With the introduction of ${\mathrm{NH}}_{4}{\mathrm{PF}}_{6}$ , on the one hand, the cationic $\mathrm{NH_{4}}^{+}$ was anticipated to effectively coordinate the bare $\mathrm{I}^{-}$ ions to inhibit the formation of I cluster defects on the bottom surface of perovskite films; on the other hand, the anionic $\mathrm{PF}_{6}{}^{-}$ was anticipated to form strong hydrogen bonds with formamidinium $(\mathrm{FA}^{+})$ or methylammonium $(\mathrm{MA}^{+})$ at $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/$ perovskite interface to reduce cation migration. Moreover, the coordination bond formed between $\mathrm{~F~}$ atoms and $S\mathrm{n}^{4+}$ in $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ film could effectively reduce the oxygen vacancy defect.
In order to verify this inference, the surface chemical states of $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ films with and without $\mathrm{NH}_{4}\mathrm{PF}_{6}$ -modified were accessed by the $\mathrm{\Delta}\mathrm{X}$ -ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) measurement (all XPS measurements were deposited on ITO). The full-scan XPS spectra of $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ films with and without $\mathrm{NH}_{4}\mathrm{PF}_{6}$ -modified are shown in Fig. S1a. Compare with the pristine sample, the $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ sample with ${\mathrm{NH}}_{4}{\mathrm{PF}}_{6}$ -modified exhibited F 1s peak, confirming the successful introduction of $\mathrm{NH}_{4}\mathrm{PF}_{6}$ in the full device (pure $\mathrm{NH}_{4}\mathrm{PF}_{6}$ is shown in Fig. S1b). Furthermore, for F 1s spectra of the $\mathrm{NH}_{4}\mathrm{PF}_{6}$ -modified $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ sample, two peaks located at 687.25 and $684.49\ \mathrm{eV}$ , corresponding to $\mathrm{~F~}$ in $\mathrm{PF}_{6}^{-}$ and Sn-F bond, respectively, were obviously observed (Fig. 1b) [44]. For pristine $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ , the Sn $3\mathrm{d}_{5/2}$ and Sn $3\mathrm{d}_{3/2}$ characteristic peaks were located at 486.28 and $494.69\ \mathrm{eV}$ , respectively (Figs. 1c, S1c). After $\mathrm{NH}_{4}\mathrm{PF}_{6}$ modification, the peaks of Sn $3\mathrm{d}_{5/2}$ $(486.97\ \mathrm{eV})$ and Sn $3\mathrm{d}_{3/2}$ $(495.43\ \mathrm{eV})$ both shifted to higher binding energies, suggesting the strong chemical interaction between $S\mathrm{n}^{4+}$ and $\mathrm{PF}_{6}{}^{-}$ on the surface of $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ film. Meanwhile, the $\mathrm{NH_{4}P F_{6}}$ - modified $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ film also exists Sn-F bond, which is located at $488.42\ \mathrm{eV}$ . The existence of Sn-F bond may indicate that oxygen vacancy in $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ will be filled by F atom. In order to confirm whether F in $\mathrm{NH_{4}P F_{6}}$ can effectively fill the oxygen vacancy defects on the surface of $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ film, we carried out XPS of $\mathrm{~o~}$ 1s spectra for the pristine $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{NH_{4}P F_{6}}$ - modified $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ films. As exhibited in Fig. 1d, the lattice oxygen $\Gamma(0_{\mathrm{L}})$ and vacancy oxygen $(\mathrm{O}_{\mathrm{V}})$ peaks of the O 1s peak of pristine $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{NH}_{4}\mathrm{PF}_{6}$ -modified $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ films are both located at 530.93 and $532.26\ \mathrm{eV}$ . It has been reported that the large number of oxygen vacancies in $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ films can negatively affect the efficiency and stability of PSC [44]. Therefore, we compared the peak area ratio $(R_{\mathrm{OV}})$ of $\mathrm{\DeltaO_{V}}$ in $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ films. The $R_{\mathrm{OV}}$ values were calculated according to the equation $R_{\mathrm{OV}}=S_{\mathrm{OV}}/(S_{\mathrm{OL}}+S_{\mathrm{OV}})$ , where $S_{\mathrm{OL}}$ and $S_{\mathrm{OV}}$ represent the peak areas of $\mathrm{O}_{\mathrm{L}}$ and $\mathrm{\DeltaO_{V}}$ , respectively. The $R_{\mathrm{OV}}$ of $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ with and without $\mathrm{NH}_{4}\mathrm{PF}_{6}$ modification was calculated to be 0.12 and 0.16, respectively, indicating that the $\mathrm{\DeltaO_{V}}$ was effectively filled by $\mathrm{NH}_{4}\mathrm{PF}_{6}$ . Furthermore, as shown in Fig. 1e, we confirmed the interaction of $\mathrm{I}^{-}$ with $\mathrm{NH_{4}^{+}}$ in $\mathrm{NH_{4}P F_{6}}$ by Fourier transform infrared spectroscopy (FTIR), the peak corresponding to the $\mathsf{N\mathrm{-}H}$ stretching vibration mode in pristine ${\mathrm{NH}}_{4}{\mathrm{PF}}_{6}$ was detected at 2851.57 and $2920.67~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , respectively. After $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ being added, the N–H characteristic peak was shifted, and the peak was located at 2852.43 and $2923.41~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ , respectively, illustrating that ${\mathrm{NH}}_{4}{\mathrm{PF}}_{6}$ could effectively form hydrogen bond $(\mathrm{N\mathrm{-}H\mathrm{-}\mathrm{\cdot}I)}$ with the undercoordinated $\Gamma^{-}$ defects. Moreover, to further confirm other interactions between perovskite buried interface and ${\mathrm{NH}}_{4}{\mathrm{PF}}_{6}$ passivation layer, we tested the liquid-state ${}^{19}\mathrm{F}$ nuclear magnetic resonance (NMR) spectrum. As shown in Fig. S2a, compared with the pure $\mathrm{NH_{4}P F_{6}}$ , the $\mathrm{~F~}$ signal in $\mathrm{NH_{4}P F_{6}}+\mathrm{FAI}$ has an obvious chemical shift from - $-73.44\times10^{-6}$ and - $71.56\times10^{-6}$ to $\phantom{0}-73.21\ \times$ ${{10}^{-6}}$ and $-71.33\times10^{-6}$ , respectively. Therefore, the hydrogen bond between $\mathrm{FA^{+}/M A^{+}}$ and $\mathrm{PF}_{6}{}^{-}$ can be confirmed. In short, the ${\mathrm{NH}}_{4}{\mathrm{PF}}_{6}$ can passivate the defects of perovskite layer and $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ layer by anions and cations of $\mathrm{NH_{4}^{+}}$ and $\mathrm{PF}_{6}{}^{-}$ , respectively.
To obtain the energy levels of ${\mathrm{NH}}_{4}{\mathrm{PF}}_{6}$ modified- $\mathbf{S}\mathbf{n}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ and pristine $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ films, we used ultraviolet photoelectron spectroscopy (UPS) to test. As shown in Fig. S2b, c, based on the formula Fermi levels $(E_{\mathrm{F}})=h\nu{-}E_{\mathrm{cut-off}}$ 1 $h\nu=21.2\mathrm{~eV}$ ), Fermi levels of the ${\mathrm{NH}}_{4}{\mathrm{PF}}_{6}$ modified$\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ and pristine $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ films are calculated by the secondary electron cutoff to be 3.87 and $3.74~\mathrm{eV}$ , respectively. Meanwhile, the energy difference between $E_{\mathrm{F}}$ and valence band maximum (VBM) levels is 4.08 and $3.97\ \mathrm{eV}$ , respectively, and the VBM of the ${\mathrm{NH}}_{4}{\mathrm{PF}}_{6}$ modified- $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ and pristine $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ films are - 7.95 and - $-7.71\ \mathrm{eV}$ , respectively. Combined with the band gap of $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ film, the conduction band (CBM) of ${\mathrm{NH}}_{4}{\mathrm{PF}}_{6}$ modified- $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ and pristine $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ films are further determined to be - 3.81 and $-\ 3.57\ \mathrm{eV}$ , respectively, and the obtained band map is shown in Fig. S2d. Owing to the ${\mathrm{NH}}_{4}{\mathrm{PF}}_{6}$ modified- $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ has a lower CBM content relative to the perovskite layer (CBM $\approx$ $4.05\ \mathrm{eV},$ ), there is less energy hindered in the electron transfer process [22].
In order to investigate the effect of ${\mathrm{NH}}_{4}{\mathrm{PF}}_{6}$ modification on the quality of $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ films, we examined the pristine $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ films and ${\mathrm{NH}}_{4}{\mathrm{PF}}_{6}$ -modified $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ films by using scanning electron microscopy (SEM) and $\mathrm{\DeltaX}$ -ray diffraction (XRD) measurements. The top-view SEM images of $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ thin films without and with ${\mathrm{NH}}_{4}{\mathrm{PF}}_{6}$ modification are shown in Fig. S3a, b, respectively. Compare to the pristine $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ thin film, we observed that the $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ film with $\mathrm{NH_{4}P F_{6}}$ modification became more denser and less pinhole. Furthermore, compared with the pristine thin film, the typical (110) diffraction peak of $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ grains of ${\mathrm{NH}}_{4}{\mathrm{PF}}_{6}$ -modified $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ films become much sharper (Fig. S4). It signifies that the quality of $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ films with $\mathrm{NH_{4}P F_{6}}$ -modified could be effectively improved.
Besides the effective passivation of $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ , the introduction of ${\mathrm{NH}}_{4}{\mathrm{PF}}_{6}$ also greatly affected the morphological of perovskite film. Figure 2a, b shows SEM images of perovskite films based on pristine $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ with ${\mathrm{NH}}_{4}{\mathrm{PF}}_{6}$ modification, respectively. More uniform perovskite film with larger average grain size was obtained after depositing on $\mathrm{NH}_{4}\mathrm{PF}_{6}$ -modified $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ as compared with the perovskite film based on pristine $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ . The average grain size of the perovskite film after ${\mathrm{NH}}_{4}{\mathrm{PF}}_{6}$ modification is $0.38~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ , which is better than that of the reference perovskite film $(0.28~\upmu\mathrm{m})$ (Fig. 2c). From the atomic force microscopy (AFM) measurement, the perovskite film deposited on $\mathrm{NH_{4}P F_{6}}$ -modified $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ showed a lower root-mean-square (RMS) roughness of $26.5~\mathrm{nm}$ (Fig. 2d, e), which is beneficial for improving the fill factor (FF) of PSCs. As shown in Fig. 2f, to further confirm whether the ${\mathrm{NH}}_{4}{\mathrm{PF}}_{6}$ additive could improve the crystallinity of perovskite films, XRD patterns of the perovskite films deposited on pristine $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{NH}_{4}\mathrm{PF}_{6}$ -modified $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ have been conducted. Compared with the reference perovskite film, the perovskite crystalline was effectively improved by $\mathrm{NH}_{4}\mathrm{PF}_{6}$ -modified $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ , which was further demonstrated by the enhanced spectral absorption intensity of perovskite film deposited on $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ with $\mathrm{NH_{4}P F_{6}}$ -modified in UV–Vis measurement (Fig. S5).
Finally, by fabricating ITO glass/pristine $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ (or ${\mathrm{NH}}_{4}{\mathrm{PF}}_{6}$ -modified $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\mathrm{,}$ )/perovskite/Spiro-OMeTAD/Au devices, we investigated the positive effect of ${\mathrm{NH}}_{4}{\mathrm{PF}}_{6}$ on the photovoltaic performance of the devices. Figure 3a shows SEM image of the device section with $\mathrm{NH}_{4}\mathrm{PF}_{6}$ modification. It can be roughly estimated that the thickness of perovskite layer is about $500~\mathrm{{nm}}$ . The best-performing current–voltage $\left(J-V\right)$ curves and corresponding photovoltaic parameters for devices without and with ${\mathrm{NH}}_{4}{\mathrm{PF}}_{6}$ modification, respectively shown in Fig. 3b and Table 1. The best device with ${\mathrm{NH}}_{4}{\mathrm{PF}}_{6}$ modification exhibited a PCE of $21.11\%$ with short-circuit current density $(J_{\mathrm{SC}})$ of $23.88\mathrm{\mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ of $1.11\mathrm{~V~}$ , and FF of $79.6\%$ , while the device based on pristine $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ showed an inferior PCE of $18.46\%$ with $J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ of $23.39\mathrm{\mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ of $1.05\mathrm{~V~}$ , and FF of $75.2\%$ . Moreover, the average $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ and $J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ of the ${\mathrm{NH}}_{4}{\mathrm{PF}}_{6}$ -modified device were about $50~\mathrm{mV}$ and $3\%$ higher, respectively, compared with the device based on pristine $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ (Fig. S6a, b). The addition of ${\mathrm{NH}}_{4}{\mathrm{PF}}_{6}$ to restrain non-radiative recombination and improve the quality of perovskite films, thus improving the $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ and FF of the device. In addition, the hysteresis index (HI) value of the device based on pristine $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ with $\mathrm{NH_{4}P F_{6}}$ - modified was calculated to be 0.044 and 0.013, respectively, indicating that the $\mathrm{NH_{4}P F_{6}}$ -modified device could improve interfacial charge transfer. In addition, the ${\mathrm{NH}}_{4}{\mathrm{PF}}_{6}$ -modified device PCE with different concentration is shown in Fig. S7. When the concentration of ${\mathrm{NH}}_{4}{\mathrm{PF}}_{6}$ increases from 0 (reference) to $0.2~\mathrm{mg{\cdotml}^{-1}}$ , PCE has a significant improvement. However, as the concentration further increases, the device efficiency decreases due to the excessive ${\mathrm{NH}}_{4}{\mathrm{PF}}_{6}$ at the interface can weaken the extraction and transport of electrons. As shown in Fig. 3c, the $J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ was verified by incident photon-to-electron conversion efficiency (IPCE) spectra measurement. The integrated $J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ from IPCE for the devices based on pristine $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ with $\mathrm{NH_{4}P F_{6}}$ -modified was 22.96 and $23.35\mathrm{\mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , respectively, which matched well with the $J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ measured from characteristic $J{-}V$ curves. Additionally, Fig. 3d shows the steady-state power outputs at the maximum power point (MPP) of the device based on pristine $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ with $\mathrm{NH}_{4}\mathrm{PF}_{6}$ -modified were recorded as $17.94\%$ and $20.21\%$ , respectively.
To investigate the remarkable photovoltaic performance of the device, we took the steady-state photoluminescence (PL) and time-resolved PL (TRPL) spectra of the perovskite films and $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/$ perovskite films without and with ${\mathrm{NH}}_{4}{\mathrm{PF}}_{6}$ modification. The PL intensity of perovskite films deposited on glass with ${\mathrm{NH}}_{4}{\mathrm{PF}}_{6}$ modification is 2 times higher than that of perovskite films deposited on glass without ${\mathrm{NH}}_{4}{\mathrm{PF}}_{6}$ modification, revealing that the $\mathrm{NH_{4}P F_{6}}$ modification effectively reduced trap states and suppressed non-radiative recombination (Fig. 3e, f). Moreover, the PL quenching intensity of the $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/$ perovskite film with $\mathrm{NH_{4}P F_{6}}$ -modified was significantly weaker than that of the pristine $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/$ perovskite film. After calculation, the PL fluorescence quenching rates of the perovskite films based on pristine $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ with $\mathrm{NH_{4}P F_{6}}$ modification were calculated to be $61\%$ and $87\%$ , respectively, indicating that $\mathrm{NH}_{4}\mathrm{PF}_{6}$ can effectively enhance the electron transfer efficiency. Furthermore, the TRPL spectroscopy provides more information on charge dynamics by a bi-exponential fitted parameters modification shown in Table S1. The charge recombination lifetime $(\tau_{\mathrm{avg}})$ increased from 315.5 to 536.2 ns after perovskite deposited on $\mathrm{NH}_{4}\mathrm{PF}_{6}$ -modified glass, and the $\tau_{\mathrm{avg}}$ of the $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/$ perovskite film with ${\mathrm{NH}}_{4}{\mathrm{PF}}_{6}$ modification decreased from 91.0 to 68.2 ns, which further indicates that the incorporation of $\mathrm{NH}_{4}\mathrm{PF}_{6}$ could effectively suppress trap-assisted non-radiative recombination in perovskite films, leading to higher $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ and FF.
To further investigate the effect of $\mathrm{NH_{4}P F_{6}}$ on electronic properties of the PSCs, we calculated the trap-state density $(N_{\mathrm{trap}})$ of the perovskite films based on pristine $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ with ${\mathrm{NH}}_{4}{\mathrm{PF}}_{6}$ -modified by space charge limited current (SCLC) technique for the electron-only device with the structure of $\mathrm{ITO}/\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/$ perovskite $\mathrm{\Delta/PC_{61}B M/A g}$ . The voltage corresponding to the kinking point between the ohmic region and the trap-filled-limited (TFL) region is called the trap-filled limit voltage $(V_{\mathrm{TFL}})$ (Fig. 4a). The $V_{\mathrm{TFL}}$ of devices deposited on pristine $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ with ${\mathrm{NH}}_{4}{\mathrm{PF}}_{6}$ -modified is 0.29 and $0.16\mathrm{~V~}$ , respectively. According to the equation of $N_{\mathrm{trap}}=(2\varepsilon\varepsilon_{0}V_{\mathrm{TFL}})/(\mathrm{e}L^{2})$ , the calculated results showed that the $N_{\mathrm{trap}}$ of perovskite films based on pristine $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ with $\mathrm{NH_{4}P F_{6}}$ -modified was $2.05\times10^{15}$ and $1.12\times10^{15}\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ , respectively, which implies that ${\mathrm{NH}}_{4}{\mathrm{PF}}_{6}$ could effectively passivate the buried interface defects of the perovskite films.
To understand the impact of $\mathrm{NH_{4}P F_{6}}$ on charge transport/recombination at interface of $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\prime$ perovskite, we future investigated the electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) of PSCs. Figure 4b shows the Nyquist plots of the PSCs based on pristine $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ with ${\mathrm{NH}}_{4}{\mathrm{PF}}_{6}$ modification. Compared with the reference device, the significantly increased recombination resistance $(R_{\mathrm{rec}})$ for the devices based on $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ with ${\mathrm{NH}}_{4}{\mathrm{PF}}_{6}$ modification, indicating that the charge recombination was remarkably suppressed and electron extraction was significantly improved at the $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/$ perovskite interface, which ultimately contribute to an increased $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ . This result conforms to the previous $J{-}V$ curves and trap-state density analysis. Moreover, for in-depth understanding the effect of
ETL on the charge transport/extraction at the interface of $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}.$ /perovskite, the alteration of built-in potential $(V_{\mathrm{bi}})$ in the full device was further researched. The value of $V_{\mathrm{bi}}$ was obtained by Mott–Schottky (M–S) measurements. the PSCs based on $\mathrm{NH_{4}P F_{6}}$ -modified $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ exhibited much higher $V_{\mathbf{bi}}$ of $0.85\mathrm{~V~}$ as compared to the devices with pristine $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ $(0.68{\mathrm{~V}})$ , which implies the enhancement of charge accumulation/extraction at the interface of $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/$ perovskite interface (Fig. 4c). To further demonstrate that ${\mathrm{NH}}_{4}{\mathrm{PF}}_{6}$ could improve the interfacial charge extraction efficiency, it is very necessary to study light intensity dependent of PSCs. Figure 4d presents the dependence of $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ on light intensity. The $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ slope values of the reference PSCs were $1.92k T/q$ , while the device based on $\mathrm{NH_{4}P F_{6}}$ -modified $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ exhibited a much smaller slope value of $1.35~k T/q$ . The slope value closer to $1.00~k T/q$ for the $\mathrm{NH_{4}P F_{6}}$ -modified device suggests that Shockley–Read–Hall recombination was significantly inhibited after ${\mathrm{NH}}_{4}{\mathrm{PF}}_{6}$ modification. Meanwhile, Fig. S8 shows the dependence of
$J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ on light intensity, the $\boldsymbol{\mathfrak{X}}$ value for the device based on $\mathrm{NH_{4}P F_{6}}$ -modified $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ is much closer to 1 compared to the device based on pristine $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ , which manifested that the interfacial charge transport/extraction was significantly improved after ${\mathrm{NH}}_{4}{\mathrm{PF}}_{6}$ modifying the $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ layer (according to the equation of $J_{\mathrm{SC}}=I^{\alpha}$ , where $I$ stands for light intensity and $\alpha$ represents the exponential factor). These results are in good agreement with increased $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ and FF.
To better understand the effect of ${\mathrm{NH}}_{4}{\mathrm{PF}}_{6}$ on device stability, the devices were stored in an air environment with a relative humidity (RH) of $35\%\pm5\%$ without encapsulated. As shown in Fig. 5a, the PSCs deposited on ${\mathrm{NH}}_{4}{\mathrm{PF}}_{6}$ -modified $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ still retained $80\%$ of its initial PCE after $1080\mathrm{~h~}$ of aging, while the PSCs deposited on pristine $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ only maintained about $20.6\%$ under the same conditions. Meanwhile, the thermal stability $(60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ test showed similar results (Fig. 5b). After thermal aging for $100\mathrm{~h~}$ , the PSCs based on $\mathrm{NH_{4}P F_{6}}$ -modified $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ retained $80.1\%$ of its initial PCE, which was much higher than the device based on pristine $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ $(50\%)$ . Furthermore, as shown in Fig. 5c, the $\mathrm{NH_{4}P F_{6}}$ -modified device also shows better illumination stability, maintaining $88.2\%$ of its initial PCE after 100-h 1 sun irradiation in the ambient air atmosphere, while the reference device retained $64.5\%$ of its initial PCE. The improvement of the stability of $\mathrm{NH}_{4}\mathrm{PF}_{6}$ -modified device can be mainly attributed to the improvement of the quality of perovskite film, which is supported by water contact angle and aging of perovskite film measurement results. Figure 5d exhibits the morphological changes of the perovskite films $\mathrm{(RH>90\%}$ ). The perovskite with pristine $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ gradually changed from black to yellow during the aging process. However, the perovskite film deposited on ${\mathrm{NH}}_{4}{\mathrm{PF}}_{6}$ -modified $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ still maintained the initial black after aging for $96\mathrm{~h~}$ . Furthermore, Fig. S9 shows that the angle increased from $61.8^{\circ}$ to $87.0^{\circ}$ after perovskite deposited on $\mathrm{NH_{4}P F_{6}}$ -modified $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ . Therefore, on the one hand, the introduction of ${\mathrm{NH}}_{4}{\mathrm{PF}}_{6}$ can passivate the defects of perovskite and slow down ion migration; on the other hand, it can improve the crystal quality and increase the water contact angle of perovskite film, effectively blocking the water vapor entry and thus improving the stability of the device. Moreover, we further fabricated larger areas PSC (active area ${\bf\mu}=1{\bf\sigma}$ $\mathrm{cm}^{2},$ , the device with $\mathrm{NH_{4}P F_{6}}$ -modified exhibited the best PCE of $17.38\%$ , while the device based on pristine $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ showed an inferior PCE of $15.25\%$ (Fig. 6a, b). The results demonstrate the successful application of $\mathrm{NH_{4}P F_{6}}$ -modified strategy on large-scale thin films.
## 3 Conclusion
In short, we have reported a significative strategy to passivate the defects between perovskite and ETL interface with ${\mathrm{NH}}_{4}{\mathrm{PF}}_{6}$ salt. The ${\mathrm{NH}}_{4}{\mathrm{PF}}_{6}$ effectively passivated the oxygen defects on the surface of $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ film, optimized the energy level arrangement between $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ and perovskite, enhanced the quality of perovskite film, and improved the charge transport at the $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/$ perovskite interface. Thus, the PSCs based on $\mathrm{NH_{4}P F_{6}}$ -modified $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ achieved a best PCE of $21.11\%$ with reduced $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ loss, which is a relatively high result for $\mathrm{(FAPbI_{3})_{0.85}(M A P b B r_{3})_{0.15}}$ composition as active layer in PSC. In addition, the PCE of large area $(1~\mathrm{cm}^{2})$ PSCs with $\mathrm{NH_{4}P F_{6}}$ -modification reached $17.38\%$ . Meanwhile, the unencapsulated $\mathrm{NH_{4}P F_{6}}$ -modified PSC retained about $80\%$ of the initial PCE after $1080\mathrm{~h~}$ aging in ambient air with a RH of $35\%\pm5\%$ , showing a good humidity stability. Therefore, this work provides an avenue to modulate the buried interface to achieve highly efficient and stable $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ -based n-i-p planar PSCs.
